-- title:
--   Indices inside a segment
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a formal proof that edit distance on binary words is far from $\ell_1$: for some $c>0$ and all large $d$ there are sets of equal-length binary words of length at most $d$ whose least $\ell_1$ distortion under edit distance is at least $\exp\bigl(c\sqrt{\log d\,\log\log d}\bigr)$. Words over a large alphabet are built recursively along a finite tree, with states in a product of cyclic groups. A Fourier argument shows that every $1$-Lipschitz map into $\ell_1$ moves a fixed translation little on average, while longest-common-subsequence estimates keep that translation far in edit distance. A random binary block code transfers the bound to binary words. Let $x$ be a word and consider the segment of $x$ starting at position $s$ with width $w$, i.e. the word obtained by dropping the first $s$ letters and keeping the next $w$.
--
--   1. **Segment index.** A position $i$ of $x$ with
--   $$
--   s\le i<s+w
--   $$
--   corresponds to the position $i-s$ of that segment. This reindexing transports matchings of whole words into matchings of segments.
